  "worked_solution": "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.45$ | $0.55$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function for $Y$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.45$ | $0.55$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
